00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.690
It takes a collaboration
such as these

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.600
to animate the vision
of artists like Nancy,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.890
and I'm very grateful for
everyone's participation.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.190
"Botanical Ghosts" is
a multipart exploration

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.165
of the history buried in
the soil of Tela, Honduras.

00:02:33.165 --> 00:02:37.680
Nancy Valla her
website to transport us

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.100
to a ghostly place,
where the Earth itself

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.170
holds dark secrets of
colonialism and power.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:51.640
The plants in this place and
the bodies buried under the soil

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:57.620
call to us and especially to
Nancy to tell their story.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.440
Nancy's vision makes
poetry of the link

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:04.070
between human domination
and environmental ruin.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.980
Her deep quality
of perception links

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.310
the present to many
layers of the past,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.950
the heart to the
scientific mind,

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:18.190
and human bodies to the biology
of the Earth that holds us.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:22.720
Nancy makes potent use
of research-based artwork

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.990
and illuminates the
importance of emotion

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:32.140
to tell stories, both
historical and scientific.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:37.510
I'm very pleased to
introduce Nancy Valladares.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.460
Thank you so much, Sarah,
for the introduction,

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:46.190
and to everyone who is here
and made this possible.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:50.170
My collaborators, I want to
say thank you one more time,

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:55.900
and to the Wiesner Gallery
for all their support and--

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.200
yeah, for the opportunity
to once again gather,

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.010
even though we're in a moment
where that's not possible.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.890
But I'm really, really
happy to see everyone

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.170
and a lot of loved
ones and colleagues

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.590
here as well, but yeah.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:16.018
I feel-- thank you
Shannon Rose [AUDIO OUT]

00:04:16.018 --> 00:04:18.860
for the introduction.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.540
And I wanted to just
talk really briefly

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:30.280
about sort of the impetus
behind this project and sort of

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.740
like a kind of
more personal lens

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.610
as to where a lot of these
ideas kind of came from.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:40.150
So during my time as a student
at MIT'S program in culture

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.810
and technology, I spent most
of my time doing research

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:46.540
in this particular site
called Lancetilla Botanical

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:51.820
Gardens, what used to be a
botanical experimental station

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:58.510
that was founded in 1923 or
24 by United Fruit Company.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.850
And I first stumbled upon the
story of a British botanist

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:09.010
and archaeologist, Dorothy
Popenoe, in that site in 2017,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.800
I believe.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.140
And so I spent a lot
of time connect--

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:19.030
making these historical
threads and connections,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.360
trying to really
understand what was buried

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.340
beneath that particular site.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.760
And I felt that that place
function as a monument

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.180
to the legacy of American
interventionism in the region.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.310
And it was a nexus
of several forces

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:40.460
that shape Honduran political
ecologies and biospheres

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.610
for decades to come.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:46.800
And so Dorothy Popenoe,
as a character,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.390
you really felt her
presence in the garden.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:52.620
You do still to these days.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.720
She haunts visitors with
her almost-biblical demise

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.300
IN the story of
how she perished,

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.170
which was eating a fruit
of the ackee, which

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:05.820
is this fruit that you see
here in the background.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.740
And there was very little
was actually written

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.940
about her work in the site, even
though she did a lot of really

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.100
great contributions.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.590
And she was a great
scholar, archaeologist,

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.230
and botanist in her own
right, but it was sort of

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:23.130
hidden behind the
larger narrative

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.400
and her husband Wilson
Popenoe's career as a botanist.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.050
And I wanted to-- first
as an introduction,

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:36.200
I'm going to start
sharing my screen.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.110
And I would like to share an
excerpt of the film as a--

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:41.428
to serve as an introduction.

00:06:41.428 --> 00:06:45.919
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:47.915
[EEK SOUND]

00:06:49.911 --> 00:06:58.394
[EEK SOUND]

00:06:58.394 --> 00:07:00.390
[EEK SOUND]

00:07:04.881 --> 00:07:05.879
[EEK SOUND]

00:07:07.376 --> 00:07:08.374
[EEK SOUND]

00:07:10.869 --> 00:07:12.366
[EEK SOUND]

00:07:13.863 --> 00:07:15.360
[EEK SOUND]

00:07:19.352 --> 00:07:20.849
[EEK SOUND]

00:07:33.324 --> 00:07:44.025
[INSECTS CHIRPING]

00:07:44.025 --> 00:07:48.300
I still do not know whose
ghost you hope to become.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.250
In your letters to
your sister, you

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:54.430
write about all the things you
did for love and out of love.

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:57.210
I read them and reread them
until the meaning of words

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.240
dissolved, and your
handwriting became a cipher

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.780
for recovering all the
things we have forgotten.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.250
In my roundabout
pursuit of you, I

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:10.610
found myself haunted
by your words,

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.100
by the things that we never
recorded or documented,

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:19.560
but whose absence
we clearly felt.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:23.190
I asked you once if you thought
that soil contained memories.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.230
You answered, "If
it could remember,

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:30.210
then we would eat all the
lifetimes it had consumed."

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.395
In your time at Kew Gardens,
you often talked about freedom.

00:08:33.395 --> 00:08:36.030
Your inquisitive mind,
your rebellious streak,

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:39.539
always got you into trouble.

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:43.380
I remember because I often felt
you left me out on purpose,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:44.850
like the things we
had shared moved

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:47.040
to a quiet moment
in the background,

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.230
and only the object of
your current obsession

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.770
could ever draw your
attention at any given time.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:38.530
What things should be on Earth?

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:41.560
What should be allowed to
rest beneath the ground?

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.390
What do we preserve?

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:47.030
If the soil remembers, why do
we need to dig, taxonomize,

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:47.530
and catalog.

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:48.113
[END PLAYBACK]

00:09:48.113 --> 00:09:50.540
I'm going to pause
the video there.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.310
You can watch the rest
of the short in the site.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.960
It's available there.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:00.760
I'm just going to sort of give
you guys a little bit more

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:04.180
of the background into
some of this material,

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:07.330
and then go through a
walk-through of the site

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:09.040
so we can kind of
experience it together.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:17.140
So when I return
to Popenoe house

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:22.030
for the second time in 2018,
I sat near the Popenoe house

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:24.760
and watched a group of bees
perform a beautiful dance

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.680
around yellow hibiscus.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:30.270
Dilapidated and in disrepair,
the wooden building

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.460
murmured all the things
rendered banal by history.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.130
I felt the same way that
often accompanies monuments

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:40.410
to violence, settled like
a blanket on my shoulders.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.230
Not too far from Popenoe
house, surrounded by a fence

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.770
and dotted with several danger
signs, Lancetilla houses

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.490
two specimens of blighia
sapido, also known

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.640
as the ackee tree, which
produces a pear-like fruit

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.000
that if it's eaten before it
ripens or is improperly cooked,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.790
it induces a severe
hypoglycemia,

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:02.790
or known as Jamaican
vomiting sickness.

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:05.340
And from what we know
and read in several books

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.190
and biographical notes,
Dorothy Popenoe died in 19--

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:12.240
in December 1932 after eating
improperly cooked ackee.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.540
I spent quite some time weaving
Dorothy's life and her work

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.840
with the transatlantic voyage
of the ackee, whose migration

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.260
reveals the ongoing extraction
of human labor and resources

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.810
at the service of empire.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.570
This route crossed through the
Middle Passage from West Africa

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:33.060
and made its way to several
islands in the Caribbean,

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:36.690
specifically Jamaica, where
it became a staple food

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:37.910
in the cooking of the region.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.420
Eventually, it made its
way to Lancetilla's part

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.270
of experiments and propagation
of tropical species

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:49.630
for United Fruit Company.

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:52.330
And as I was doing
my research, going

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:56.200
through several different
archives and sources,

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.210
it was clear to me that
a narrative was forming

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.310
from all these missing pieces.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:02.560
And there are
deliberate silences

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.610
and political
occlusions And I was

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.940
drawing from collections at
Harvard Peabody Museum, Hunt

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.310
Institute for Botanical
Documentation,

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:16.450
at Carnegie Mellon University,
at the Royal Kew gardens,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.270
among others, where
I sought to create

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.810
a new speculative archive
called "A Dedicated

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:26.740
Mechanism Of Forgetting,"
which contains

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.870
a collection of letters,
documents, and images

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.270
that were compiled over
the period of two years

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:34.120
that I was working on
"Botanical Ghosts."

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:37.390
So now I'm going
to sort of go ahead

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:42.460
and give us a very brief tour
of the website experience,

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:47.290
so that you all can
go after the talk

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.780
and experience it on your own.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:54.830
And once again, I wanted to
thank my collaborator and dear

00:12:54.830 --> 00:13:00.860
friend, Sophie Loloi, who
worked for a long time

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:01.800
to make this happen.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.820
I would not have been able
to do this without her.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.690
And so the way that the
website is structured

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:14.780
is sort of like a
book, several chapters

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:20.180
kind of comprise this particular
web iteration of the project.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.760
So you're able to sort
of experience the film,

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:27.020
and then also enter the archive,
where a lot of the documents

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:30.680
and images are kind of housed.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:38.180
And the [AUDIO OUT] still
living and it's ongoing

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:40.380
and more and more
things are being added,

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:44.030
so please check
back often to see.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:48.640
[INAUDIBLE] like I could kind of
show you what that looks like.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.130
And the archive is
interactive which

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:57.100
means that things can be kind
of reconfigured and rearranged

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:03.690
and they can be coded
in different ways,

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:07.570
and different relationships
have kind of emerged from them.

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:11.610
And these are kind
of sourced from--

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.760
some of the artifacts, for
example, that Dorothy Popenoe

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:20.280
unearthed while she was
doing work at Lancetilla.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:23.760
Some of these are film stills.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:26.910
Some of these are some sort
of letter, fictional letter

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:30.810
exchanges, letters that
I wrote to Dorothy,

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:32.340
as well as some
of the photographs

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:36.370
that she took while
she was at Lancetilla.

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.850
Yeah, for now, the
archive is still growing,

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:45.180
but I'm looking
forward very much

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:49.260
to putting more of the
material that's on there,

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:54.270
and so please go ahead and
play with it and experience it.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:58.230
It's meant to be experienced
more as a photo album,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:03.840
or a little bit more intimately
than traditional sort

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:10.210
of archival form, and so, yeah.

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:13.370
I'm going to kind of go back.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:14.990
The rest of the
website is sort of

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:18.590
comprised of a series of
writings and fictions.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:24.390
They kind of tell about
sort of Dorothy's experience

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:29.660
at Lancetilla, and I wanted to
sort of read a brief excerpt

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.180
from one of the
pieces of writing.

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:39.140
I was buried near the pomeriums.

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:42.170
The Valley of Lancetilla had
been cultivated and engineered

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.900
for hundreds of years
before we arrived.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:48.740
Before it, the people who
lived here had even settled it.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:51.020
Let me tell you about
the worlds within worlds

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.900
that came from Lancetilla

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:55.940
This has always been
a place of death,

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:57.560
where bodies were
fed to the soil,

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:00.620
and then proceed to be
coming and transforming.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:03.080
In order to ensure the
formation of the acres

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:06.650
upon acres upon plantations
in the coast of Honduras,

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:08.705
we chose to accept the
death of many things.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.190
In the soggy marsh
of the plantation,

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:15.080
everything makes love.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.240
Everything consumes everything.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.910
Then, it returns and releases.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:21.830
To summon the specters
that are buried

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:24.140
in the marshlands in
the jungle requires

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.000
an act of necromancy and magic.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.940
A Specter is that which
leaves behind an imprint,

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:34.020
whose frequency cannot be sensed
with tools for quantification.

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:35.910
Rather, it requires
a sensibility

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.040
to speak to the
inanimate and the dead,

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.020
and the living things
that are considered dead,

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:42.870
but aren't, and the
persons whose animacy

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.480
has been obscured and
undermined by structures

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.150
of power and dispossession.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:52.040
To accommodate for the world
we were about to build,

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.470
we burned a billion
graves, leveled,

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:57.710
raised to the ground,
cemeteries within cemeteries

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.830
of microbial subjectivities
and vegetal life,

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:02.690
ecological networks
that must now

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:04.819
parlay an imbalance
in the systems that

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:06.349
have been disrupted.

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:09.140
The things that once
slept, now have awakened.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.420
Whose life to witness
choose to mourn?

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:13.970
And whose deaths to do
we then memorialize?

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:19.569
And so you can sort
of experience this

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:22.390
as a sort of web
version of the text.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:24.790
You can kind of go
through, and it's not

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:27.670
in any particular
chronological order,

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:32.350
but it's rather more of
a collaging of narratives

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.124
and temporalities.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:44.830
And finally, as part of these
sort of fictional exchanges

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:48.490
that I had with Dorothy,
I spent some time

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:51.760
writing letters to her
over a period of two years

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:56.710
and I felt that I sort of like
had an experience with her that

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:59.620
was across time.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:02.470
And I wanted to read to
you one of the letters

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:07.765
that I wrote to her during
my time doing research.

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:15.950
I'm going to read
it out loud now.

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:19.400
Dear Dorothy, it's strange to
be writing to you right now,

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.430
in 2020, to express wonder
at the various interests

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.460
and desires we share,
despite being cultivated

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:27.660
under different worlds.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.540
I recently visited
Peabody Museum

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:32.600
which houses the collection of
your photographs and artifacts

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:34.778
that you excavated
from Lancetilla.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:40.070
I finished my time
as student here

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:41.690
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:43.820
I think of the places
you frequented here

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:47.400
and the institutions that across
time mark both of our lives.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.720
What is the likelihood that I
would find you here as well?

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.090
I wanted to share with you
a bit of my family history

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:56.460
and my motivations behind
writing to a ghost.

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.530
As a child, mornings
in my house were

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:00.000
marked by rituals of death.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:04.470
Although no one ever explicitly
told me, they were so.

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:07.470
The cracking open of windows,
the rituals around the cutting

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.930
of hair, the portents
of the animals,

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.520
and the blooming of
rare flowers that

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:16.420
were marked with a pencil
on the kitchen calendar.

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:19.130
When it rained, we remembered.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.590
A smell of [INAUDIBLE] were
served as both [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:25.520
and a wound, a reminder of the
circumstances which brought us

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.630
to other shores and other seas.

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:31.330
It's hard sometimes to
imagine ourselves as young

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:35.860
as we were living in that house,
the one that is no longer home.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.440
We still remember the
cliffs and the hideouts,

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:41.200
memories of a childhood's
worth of mapping

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.570
the proximate and the familiar.

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:48.130
Tiny taxonomies of
rocks shells and leaves.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:49.870
Here is a favorite tree.

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:52.180
These are the best
branches to pick mangoes,

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:53.950
and that is the
best time to hear

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:56.570
the song of the luminous bird.

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:58.400
We learn from our
grandmothers to read

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:01.100
the paths of the insects
and the white moths

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.860
that are [INAUDIBLE]
moss on the guava tree.

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:06.230
We knew that April
rain brings a blanket

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.660
of insects that
crawl inside our ear

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:11.300
and the migrant ants whose
yearly visits invited

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:13.430
the winds.

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:16.190
The moth, my grandmother's
favorite prophet,

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:18.620
would often visit
with the same missive

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:21.840
to announce the
coming of an ending.

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.720
Our family, cursed with
the gift of long lifespans,

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:27.090
was deeply intimate with
a kind of mourning that

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.380
has nothing to do with burials.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.470
Only now do I realize how
so many of our daily rituals

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:36.330
were colored by the
remnants of the nonliving.

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:39.050
It's here at the crossing
of these thresholds

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:42.196
that the world of the
dead folds into my own.

00:20:42.196 --> 00:20:45.360
And so you see, dear Dorothy,
at certain times of day

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.630
we're not so far
from each other.

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:49.010
Sometimes as if you're--

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:51.140
I feel as if you're sitting ne--

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:54.090
I feel as if you're
sitting next to me.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:55.010
Until next time.

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:02.320
And this concludes sort
of my little brief tour

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:06.260
of the website and the web
experience of this project.

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:08.950
And I think, yeah, I--

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:12.130
if anyone has any questions
I don't know, Sarah.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.960
What's next, but--

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:15.340
Oh, I will chime back in.

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:16.340
Thank you so much.

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:19.600
That was fantastic
and really compelling

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.730
and I'm sure I have questions.

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:23.150
I hope other people do.

00:21:23.150 --> 00:21:26.680
So at this point, I would
love to invite people

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:29.830
either to put questions in
the chat, raise your hand,

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:33.130
or if you'd like to unmute,
come on audio or video.

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:35.050
We'd also welcome
your conversation.

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:40.580
Yeah, anyone who wants to
come on video, we welcome you.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:43.850
Just to help celebrate
Nancy and her exhibition.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.820
Well, I've got a
question [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:56.810
if other people are
still sort of letting

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:58.910
all of this marinade
in their brains.

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:01.460
There's a lot to take in
because it was so impressive.

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:05.270
So what I would love to
know a little bit more,

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:09.230
what's your personal history
with the research station?

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:12.590
Did you find it And then were
you inspired to tell the story

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:15.350
or were you first
looking to tell the story

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:17.120
and then you
discovered the station

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:19.400
and discovered Dorothy Popenoe?

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:21.350
Do you have a history in Tela?

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:23.720
Tell us some more
of this back story.

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:26.690
I actually kind of
stumbled upon it.

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:27.800
This is sort of--

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:31.160
Lancetilla's a place where
it's become a park, where

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:33.050
you can kind of go visit.

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:40.080
And it's a place where you kind
of go to in the North coast.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:41.870
I honestly didn't
know much about it

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:47.730
until I went there once with a
cousin of mine and I was very,

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:54.820
very just compelled by a
lot of things in that site.

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.290
It was very, very--

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.720
left an impression on me.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:02.740
And the more research that
I did, the deeper kind of--

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:06.460
you go into a story,
I realized actually

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:11.650
there was like a lot of
connections to our history

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:14.470
that I didn't really, really
realize until much later.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:18.700
For example, my
grandfather he worked on he

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.760
was a laborer who worked
on the railroads that

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:26.140
were used to kind
of transport fruit

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:29.140
and materials in
the North coast.

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:34.300
And it's a very common history
actually, Honduran family kind

00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:36.730
of history in Honduras
where you probably

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:42.790
had some family member who
works either in plantations

00:23:42.790 --> 00:23:47.020
or in sort of like in
some kind of agriculture.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:53.050
And I think that was
sort of a part of--

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:54.480
also, the imaginary.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:57.940
You think of Honduran
cultural identity

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:05.360
which sort of like a
fact of strange kind

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:07.640
of shame, but
simultaneous pride,

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:10.160
that Honduras was called
the first Banana Republic

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.350
in the sort of pejorative term.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:16.820
Those used to describe countries
in Central America and Latin

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:19.640
America whose soils
and territories

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:22.970
were used for,
essentially, a kind

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:26.000
of extractive
agricultural paradigm.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:36.140
And so, it's both personal, but
also like a very not uncommon,

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:40.640
common experience I think
in Honduras and other places

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:43.510
in Central America as well.

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:45.370
That's excellent.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.920
Really good answer.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:50.120
That leads me to
