00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:04.410
I had a question about
Dorothy's politics

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:08.070
as the wife of somebody employed
by the United Fruit Company.

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:10.740
To what extent did she--

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:14.400
was there any criticality
about kind of their position

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:16.620
and his being there?

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:22.030
Or was it more just
focused on her work?

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:28.770
I'm curious about how maybe was
there any tension between them

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:37.080
or perhaps even between him and
sort of his role in the public

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:42.110
or as an official role or
a private difference there.

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:43.745
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:52.330
I think there's a kind
couple of things in there

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:55.520
that I'll try to answer.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:26:00.190
So in terms of Dorothy's
politics, I think--

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:02.890
I mean I can only kind of
speculate at this point,

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:06.400
although I did spend some
time with her sort of letters

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:08.080
and her writing.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:11.660
And I do believe that there
was some tension, of course,

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:19.490
between her sort
of attempt at being

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:22.990
a botanist, an archaeologist,
a scholar in her own right,

00:26:22.990 --> 00:26:27.580
particularly at that time
and making and paving

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:31.360
her way despite the
fact that she was often

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:34.510
in the shadow of Wilson.

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:36.660
Wilson Poponoe's
kind of big name.

00:26:41.628 --> 00:26:44.170
Personally, my relationship to
her is a very complicated one.

00:26:44.170 --> 00:26:49.860
I really deeply believe that
she was complicit in all

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:52.320
of these kind of structures.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:59.910
She very much performed the
labor of empire, of capitalism,

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:04.020
of these kind of colonial
desires to collect,

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:10.020
to preserve and then to--

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:16.620
like historicizing things,
perhaps, that don't necessarily

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:17.730
need to be historicized.

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:19.050
Or perhaps they do.

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:21.690
I don't know.

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:28.640
So I think it was complicated
because she also had a very--

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:35.740
a way of thinking about life
and about the creatures,

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:37.670
the plants, the
vegetal world that she

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:39.160
was encountering in that place.

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:48.950
I think that she was
thinking about animacy

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:52.490
at a time where it was like
very precocious thinking.

00:27:52.490 --> 00:27:57.890
I think but I cannot of course
project these more contemporary

00:27:57.890 --> 00:28:01.250
notions of what that
means, particularly under

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.590
the post-humanities.

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:05.420
And so I think it's complicated.

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:07.850
I think in many
ways it's excavating

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:09.530
those questions
and those silences

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:13.680
where fiction kind of comes in.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:15.530
And so I think the
Dorothy Popenoe

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:19.550
that I'm kind of dealing
with here is a fiction.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:24.410
She is very much a character
that I've appropriated

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:29.080
in a way, and so I feel
my job as an artist,

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:34.880
I think I was here to kind
of harness that in a way

00:28:34.880 --> 00:28:37.520
that was productive maybe.

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:38.901
Or I hope was productive.

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:43.990
[AUDIO OUT] answer the question.

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:44.490
Cool.

