00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:07.600
You talked about the impression
that the park had on you.

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:09.470
Can you talk more about that?

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:11.260
What was the
attraction before you

00:29:11.260 --> 00:29:13.840
had done all of this
research and processing,

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:15.880
and what do you think
you were seeking

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:18.070
through this undertaking
and this process?

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:23.665
Yeah, thank you Sam,
for that question.

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:33.070
I don't know if--

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:37.180
I think intuition is a good
word, but I think this is--

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:44.378
that particular site was a place
that I felt contained already.

00:29:44.378 --> 00:29:45.920
You just walk in
there, you just know

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:50.510
that there's things that
are haunting that place.

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:55.300
It just feels like a place that
has a lot of stories to tell.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:01.310
I think I had a kind of
emotional experience there as

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:08.330
well because the North coast
of Honduras has become--

00:30:08.330 --> 00:30:10.940
I don't know and I can't
give you exactly percentages

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:14.030
or numbers right now
off the top of my head,

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:17.480
but has the ecologies and
the biome of that area

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:26.860
has been largely turned
into one big plantation,

00:30:26.860 --> 00:30:34.340
like a palm plantation, sort
of monocultural landscape.

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:37.970
And Lancetilla sort
of is this oasis

00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:42.530
that has incredible
biodiversity that's

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:46.370
both engineered,
but native, so it's

00:30:46.370 --> 00:30:49.190
sort of like a little protected
area that still contains

00:30:49.190 --> 00:30:52.940
the specters of what the
ecosystem was before and then

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:55.340
how it was transformed through
the introduction of all

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:58.590
these new plant species
into the region.

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:03.900
And I think that that
to me was sort of

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:06.690
like maybe the thing that--

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:09.840
the first magnet, and then
it just kind of became

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.252
a little wormhole
that I couldn't

00:31:12.252 --> 00:31:14.460
escape for like two years
and still trying to escape.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:22.150
But I think that
in terms of what

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:24.490
I was thinking through
undertaking the process,

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:27.430
I think I was looking
for a story as well.

00:31:27.430 --> 00:31:32.230
And I kind of stumbled upon
it and I found it and I--

00:31:32.230 --> 00:31:40.408
it's been in my mind for
a long time and so, yeah.

00:31:40.408 --> 00:31:40.950
I don't know.

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:44.070
Sometimes you just kind of
stumble upon those things.

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:48.550
These things can be inexplicable
in the best possible way.

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:52.950
So [? Cristiana ?]
says there's gratitude

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:56.130
for the beautiful
presentation and the website

00:31:56.130 --> 00:32:00.210
and an appreciation
for the notes you have,

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:03.300
whose life do we choose
to mourn, whose deaths do

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.130
we then memorialize?

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:07.710
And the question is
why is the format

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:12.100
of the archive important to you,
or important to this project

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:13.230
specifically?

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:16.500
It seems as though the legacy
of the archive privileges

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:19.440
certain items that
are deemed valuable

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:22.050
with the significant
absence of others,

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:26.400
and your project is very
articulate in the specter

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:28.290
from this place of absence.

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:31.320
So do you wish to take
a position in relation

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:33.870
to the archive
regarding reclaiming it,

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:35.220
criticizing it, et cetera?

00:32:37.755 --> 00:32:39.505
Yeah, thank you so
much for that question.

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:45.760
Definitely my hope,
my aspiration,

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:50.260
is to yes, to kind of
look at the archive

00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:55.700
as the kind of forum that
definitely needs to be--

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:59.570
or an attempt at being
sort of critically

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:01.730
fabulating it, to use the term.

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:07.480
Yeah.

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:11.290
Sorry, I'm getting
tongue tied here.

00:33:11.290 --> 00:33:18.380
I mean I think archives
are these forms that

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:23.570
seek to like frame or to
present a certain version

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:25.950
of a dominant narrative.

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:33.230
And I think that for me,
I was very much interested

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:38.900
in these kind of empty
spaces that you pointed out.

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:46.920
Gaps that can be filled
in through fiction

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:48.690
and speculation.

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:52.660
And I think that that is an
act of reclamation for me.

00:33:52.660 --> 00:33:55.210
I think I'm still trying
to work through that.

00:33:55.210 --> 00:33:58.080
I don't know that
necessarily it's--

00:33:58.080 --> 00:34:02.700
that it's fully fleshed
out or resolved,

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:09.550
but my hope is
that in this space

00:34:09.550 --> 00:34:13.510
and by further engaging
with it, I can reach that.

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:15.850
But yeah, I definitely
feel the archive

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:17.679
has been formulated
and reformulated

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:23.060
in so many ways
in art practices.

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:27.040
I was actually talking about
this with Sarah the other day.

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:39.699
I was thinking about also the
relationship to the family

00:34:39.699 --> 00:34:46.600
album, the more personal
and intimate archives

00:34:46.600 --> 00:34:49.810
that we all sort of built
as ways of experiencing

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:52.989
and memorializing the world.

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:54.699
Also as a kind of
counter-narrative

00:34:54.699 --> 00:35:00.290
to this more institutional
and almost hegemonic

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:02.630
form of telling history.

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:06.020
And so Dorothy's
personal album, a lot

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:08.900
of those images that are
kind of pertinent in the film

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:15.170
are drawing from her personal,
more intimate moments

00:35:15.170 --> 00:35:20.180
between her family, herself,
portraits of her loved ones.

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:22.130
And I thought that
those kind of served

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:26.600
as a weight that the
things in the archive--

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:28.820
in other archives
did not contain.

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:39.370
And that that collusion between
her more academic and yeah,

00:35:39.370 --> 00:35:42.420
more historically heavy work.

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:45.210
I think those two things were
doing something more for me.

00:35:49.640 --> 00:35:53.540
We've got another one that
definitely resonates with me.

00:35:53.540 --> 00:35:56.810
Can you talk a little bit
more about the ackee fruit,

00:35:56.810 --> 00:35:59.630
its role in your
relationship with Dorothy,

00:35:59.630 --> 00:36:06.570
and its history in the botanical
project of imperial expansion?

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:08.810
I think you're right.

00:36:08.810 --> 00:36:12.330
Yes, that is a network
that needs to be praised,

00:36:12.330 --> 00:36:15.850
so I'm going to do
my best to do that.

00:36:15.850 --> 00:36:22.390
Yes, the ackee fruit became
also like a really interesting

00:36:22.390 --> 00:36:30.500
character for me that
took on a life of its own.

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:38.300
I think that definitely part
of the challenge of this story

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:41.810
and about these
characters, particularly

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:47.430
non-human characters, is
how do you kind of try

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:54.040
to look at history from the
perspective of a fruit that

00:36:54.040 --> 00:36:59.320
is very much an anthropocentric
and probably anthropomorphic

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:00.850
desire.

00:37:00.850 --> 00:37:04.330
But Ray who asked
the question, Ray,

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:08.860
whose the person who asked the
question, my colleague, you

00:37:08.860 --> 00:37:15.770
yourself talk about the role
I guess of empathy or how

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:18.620
can these kind of
anthropomorphic desires--

00:37:18.620 --> 00:37:23.580
could they become maybe
tools through which

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:27.720
we can shift this
anthropocentrism, so for me

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:28.810
that was a question.

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:31.200
I don't know if
the form itself was

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:34.950
doing that, but as an
exercise in thinking

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.750
was really, really useful.

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:42.640
In terms of my relationship
with Dorothy and the fruit

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:44.560
and this kind of
triangulation that

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:46.690
was happening throughout
history, the fruit

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:49.330
itself was the more
deeper history.

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:57.260
It was going much further
back in time, all the way back

00:37:57.260 --> 00:38:01.070
to its roots in West
Africa and the fact

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:05.750
that it had been a staple food
for a really, really long time,

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:08.440
much longer before the
colonial project even started.

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:16.450
And I think I really like
this sort of triangulation,

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:23.290
this historical encounter, maybe
as a vector through which story

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:25.270
can be told and through
which history can

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:29.870
be challenged and reconfigured.

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:36.170
And yeah, I think that the
networks of botanical exchange

00:38:36.170 --> 00:38:39.960
that were part of
the colonial project

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:45.270
can tell us a lot about
the histories that

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:50.950
are being hidden now to this
day, particularly histories

00:38:50.950 --> 00:38:57.260
of agriculture as they
relate to resource extraction

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:03.520
through the exploitation of
Black folks in Latin America

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:06.910
and all over the Caribbean
and the United States.

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:10.510
Because plantation
histories that

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:13.720
were flourishing at
the time of slavery,

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:17.560
also made their way
through specific vectors

00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:21.730
and from the Caribbean and
all throughout Latin America.

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:23.320
And so the technology
of agriculture

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:28.120
that we have now have their
roots, as well, in plantation,

00:39:28.120 --> 00:39:30.400
these sort of
plantation ecologies.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:37.780
And that, to me, is
a really crucial lens

00:39:37.780 --> 00:39:42.010
that we had been missing for a
really long time, particularly

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:43.810
in Honduran history.

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:47.800
And so it was kind of locus
and reconfiguration I think--

00:39:50.780 --> 00:39:51.860
yeah.

00:39:51.860 --> 00:39:56.140
It's I think for me
provided an elasticity

00:39:56.140 --> 00:39:59.710
that other more dominant
perspectives of history

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:00.460
did not have.

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:06.910
So then I sort of have
a two fer for you,

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:08.520
so give me a second
because we have

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:12.510
two quite interrelated questions
from [? Samina ?] and Marissa.

00:40:12.510 --> 00:40:16.410
So firstly, you talk about
your complicated relationship

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:17.340
with Dorothy.

00:40:17.340 --> 00:40:19.680
She is a dear colleague,
but she's also

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:23.370
an agent of colonialism.

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:25.680
Can you say more
about your experience

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:28.260
of getting to know your
home through this character,

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:31.110
and relatedly, the similar
question and thought

00:40:31.110 --> 00:40:35.250
from Marissa, is in thinking
about imperial expansion,

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:39.150
it's interesting how you,
Nancy, described your project

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:41.250
as appropriating Dorothy.

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:43.650
you're very deliberate
with your diction,

00:40:43.650 --> 00:40:48.710
so can you elaborate on both
of these questions together?

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:49.430
Yeah.

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:52.950
Oh, these are spicy questions.

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:55.370
I'm going to try
and answer that.

00:40:58.780 --> 00:40:59.620
Yeah, I think--

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:04.090
Samina's actually my roommate.

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:10.240
She's in the other room
asking the spicy questions.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:11.105
Thank you so much.

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:15.920
Yeah.

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:19.880
No, I think this complicated
relationship with Dorothy

00:41:19.880 --> 00:41:22.940
is also I think maybe a mirror
of my complicated relationship

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:26.060
[INAUDIBLE] to
home, to Honduras.

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:34.530
It's I think it was also a
very personal and deeply maybe

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:37.470
solipsistic exercise
because I was also

00:41:37.470 --> 00:41:45.610
working through identity, being
part of the kind of diaspora

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:50.760
of Central American immigrants
in the United States.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:55.650
And I think that those
complicated relationships

00:41:55.650 --> 00:42:02.640
and positions and politics
also manifest themselves

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:04.380
in me as well.

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:09.600
I think that that is
also a recognition that--

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:15.330
the complexity I think of
being also happens to this day,

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:19.460
to me, to other folks who might
be members of the diaspora.

00:42:22.330 --> 00:42:29.220
And so maybe I don't know
if it's possible for me

00:42:29.220 --> 00:42:30.270
to appropriate Dorothy.

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:39.570
I don't know if that is the
term that I would use, but yeah.

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:44.420
I mean I feel that
stories from the Northern

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:51.320
Triangle, particularly Honduras,
it's really hard to find them.

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:53.000
I think that there's
a lot of them,

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:59.630
but I feel that they are
not given as much leverage

00:42:59.630 --> 00:43:04.020
or as much weight
as they should.

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:09.310
In some ways, I think that
the history of that region

00:43:09.310 --> 00:43:12.400
has been deeply
submerged because

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:16.660
of American interventionism
in the region

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:19.510
and it continues to
be so, even though we

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:22.960
have an ongoing exodus of--

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:28.240
at this very moment
of climate refugees

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:33.100
that are fleeing the region
due to ecological collapse

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:38.290
through corruption, through a
lot of different reasons, but--

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:41.360
and I'm not saying
that this is--

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:46.920
I'm not trying to make
[AUDIO OUT] reason,

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:49.890
but I'm certain that
these things can

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:52.280
be connected to that, so.

00:43:55.070 --> 00:43:56.570
Hi.

00:43:56.570 --> 00:43:58.880
Congratulations, Nancy.

00:43:58.880 --> 00:44:00.110
Thank you.

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:01.820
Thank you so much [INAUDIBLE].

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:04.730
I have a little
question that has

00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:09.230
to do with the archival
impulse in your work as well,

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:15.590
building on someone
else's question earlier.

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:19.610
And the way your platform
sort of generates archives out

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:21.170
of archives.

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:22.940
I was wondering if
you could expand

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:29.870
on how you conceived the
online platform as a space

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:33.500
of cultural speculation.

00:44:33.500 --> 00:44:35.990
Because the way I see
it, or the way I read it,

00:44:35.990 --> 00:44:40.010
and I've been playing around
with the website since earlier

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:43.850
and it's beautiful.

00:44:43.850 --> 00:44:46.280
I sort of see it as
a way to reconcile

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:49.850
the colonial historical
weight of your research

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:55.160
with the and sort of
the semantic reading

00:44:55.160 --> 00:45:03.140
of the archive with the properly
situated historical present.

00:45:05.810 --> 00:45:07.850
So yeah, I was just
sort of wondering

00:45:07.850 --> 00:45:14.240
if you see yourself as--

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:18.350
I've been reading lots
of [INAUDIBLE] today

00:45:18.350 --> 00:45:23.090
so thinking of the
artist as archivist

00:45:23.090 --> 00:45:25.380
versus the artist
as ethnographer,

00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:33.110
and if you sort of see yourself
in this line of thought

00:45:33.110 --> 00:45:35.030
and if it's a way
for you to reclaim

00:45:35.030 --> 00:45:36.530
these difficult histories.

00:45:36.530 --> 00:45:38.120
Sorry, my question is a little--

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:39.180
No, no, no.

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:39.755
It's great.

00:45:39.755 --> 00:45:40.505
Thank you so much.

00:45:43.700 --> 00:45:44.720
That is a good question.

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:54.660
So in some ways, what I think
I like about this position

00:45:54.660 --> 00:45:58.800
is that as an artist, you can
kind of embody different roles

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:05.230
and you're not necessarily
beholden to the forms

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:10.870
or the traditions of these
other disciplines, which

00:46:10.870 --> 00:46:15.730
is problematic, but
it's also in some ways

00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:19.920
an impulse that I
think a lot of artists

00:46:19.920 --> 00:46:21.210
kind of choose to follow.

00:46:26.210 --> 00:46:28.170
I think that that
flexibility and that kind

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:31.800
of nebulous gray area between
disciplines is one that I

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:34.310
really--

00:46:34.310 --> 00:46:36.200
and that, for me,
was very empowering.

00:46:36.200 --> 00:46:37.700
I've always I mean
I've been someone

00:46:37.700 --> 00:46:39.075
who's been interested
in history,

00:46:39.075 --> 00:46:40.980
but I'm not a historian.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:46.310
And I may follow kind of some
methodologies of research,

00:46:46.310 --> 00:46:51.755
but I'm not necessarily like
producing academic work.

00:46:51.755 --> 00:46:57.150
I'm first and foremost an
artist and filmmaker, and so--

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:02.990
Yeah.

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:03.920
I don't know.

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:05.870
It's a gray area.

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:08.420
I don't know.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:10.640
So question mark.

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:16.450
It just makes me think
of two words that

00:47:16.450 --> 00:47:18.910
keep coming to mind that
you've already mentioned

00:47:18.910 --> 00:47:23.260
in this Q&amp;A, which is
stretch and reconfigure,

00:47:23.260 --> 00:47:26.830
and I feel like I love the
fact that the archive itself

00:47:26.830 --> 00:47:29.350
is stretchable and
reconfigurable because it's

00:47:29.350 --> 00:47:34.300
like you're literally
parting apart the formerly

00:47:34.300 --> 00:47:37.330
known academic facts.

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:38.020
Yes, yes.

00:47:38.020 --> 00:47:41.440
--putting in your letter,
your photo, your encounter,

00:47:41.440 --> 00:47:43.900
your feeling and
fleshing it out.

00:47:43.900 --> 00:47:45.400
But that stretch
and reconfigure,

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:47.140
I just think that's
really powerful.

00:47:47.140 --> 00:47:47.640
Yeah.

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:51.760
I'm actually-- maybe
I'm going to use

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:56.320
this as a plug for
the incoming panel

00:47:56.320 --> 00:48:02.995
with my colleague, my colleague
Samina and Cherine Hamza.

00:48:02.995 --> 00:48:04.790
I don't know if Cherine's here.

00:48:04.790 --> 00:48:06.790
But we're actually going
to be talking precisely

00:48:06.790 --> 00:48:11.890
about this sort of like about
the experiences of artists

00:48:11.890 --> 00:48:15.940
and historians in the
archives, and so we're

00:48:15.940 --> 00:48:21.270
going to be having that
discussion sometime in March.

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:24.240
Because I feel that one of
the things that we were kind

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:28.950
of discussing together before
like the talk even happened,

00:48:28.950 --> 00:48:33.000
was that there are moments of
affect that happen while you're

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:35.040
looking through all
of this material

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:39.630
that as academics you might
not be able to express or even

00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:41.790
speculate on because
automatically

00:48:41.790 --> 00:48:47.790
that disqualifies or
[AUDIO OUT] that you're

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:49.500
trying to do as an academic.

00:48:52.450 --> 00:48:54.900
And so, the question
is then what

00:48:54.900 --> 00:49:01.470
happens to the things that
do kind of produce affect?

00:49:01.470 --> 00:49:03.470
What does it mean when
you go into an archive

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:05.420
and you kind of break down?

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:08.900
When you have an emotional
experience being there

00:49:08.900 --> 00:49:13.310
and witnessing, seeing
the material spoils,

00:49:13.310 --> 00:49:17.660
I think, that colonial
impulse for collection,

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:19.460
for organizing and classifying.

00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:26.250
And so I think that that is
where a lot of the friction

00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:29.760
is happening.

00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:30.360
Yeah.

00:49:30.360 --> 00:49:32.135
In conversation
with my colleagues

00:49:32.135 --> 00:49:33.510
I realize it's
not something that

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:35.520
just happens to the artist.

00:49:35.520 --> 00:49:39.050
It's something
that-- it's simply

00:49:39.050 --> 00:49:42.830
there's no space for
that in academia.

00:49:42.830 --> 00:49:44.120
Or maybe there should be.

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:48.740
And perhaps maybe there
should be a dedicated way

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:57.040
of addressing these other things
and making space for them.

00:49:57.040 --> 00:49:59.590
This-- oh, sorry I don't
want to talk over you.

